title op3: a directory child votes by its leaf or not at all

# What a DIRECTORY can say when it is asked to vote for its parent,
# and the fixture that separates the two halves of the answer.
#
# Off-of rebuilds /proj as /work.  Everything below is severed, and
# both children are childless directories, so neither can be believed
# to have moved on its own -- an empty directory has no voters and
# concludes nothing.  That strips the case down to one question: what
# is left for a directory to vote WITH?  Onto is quiet.
#
#   cfg -> conf   the leaf changed.  Red is dead, it has no believed
#                 move of its own, and yellow is not available to a
#                 directory at all: Definition 2.8 compares
#                 directories by attributes, so a default-attributed
#                 directory is yellow to nearly every other one and
#                 the vote would assert nothing.  Nothing reaches it.
#                 It ABSTAINS.
#
#   data -> data  the leaf did not change.  Inside the candidate pair
#                 (/proj, /work) the leaf data is equal on both sides,
#                 which is a NAME MATCH -- relativized green -- and
#                 nothing in Definition 16.3 restricts that row to
#                 files.  It VOTES.
#
# The asymmetry is the point.  Exactly one row of the triad is
# restricted to non-directories, and it says so; the name-match row
# says no such thing, and it should not, because a leaf is unique
# inside a directory whatever it happens to name.
#
# THE FIXTURE IS BUILT SO THAT ROW DECIDES THE OUTCOME.  cfg abstains
# and abstentions do not block, so data is the ONLY voter /proj has.
# If a directory child may vote by name match, /proj has one
# unanimous voter, /work is its unique target, and the move is
# believed.  If it may not, /proj has no voters at all, fullness is
# vacuous, and nothing is concluded.  One line of gold apart.
#
# That is not hypothetical.  The first draft of this lane's
# implementation denied directories the name-match row, and every one
# of the ten fixtures written before this one agreed with it anyway,
# because in each of them a file was doing the voting.  A bug that no
# fixture can see is a bug that ships.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  /proj -> /work, on data's vote alone.  Neither
#                    subdirectory is believed to have moved itself:
#                    both are childless, so both are vacuous
#   deltas:          relative to (/proj, /work), data and its
#                    neighbours keep their leaves; no pool has a lost
#                    or gained set
#   decisions:       /work reuses onto's dnode 2.  /work/data reuses
#                    onto's dnode 5 -- the pairing amendment pairs a
#                    severed child whose leaf survives under a
#                    corresponded pair.  /work/conf materializes,
#                    because cfg's leaf did not survive and so nothing
#                    pairs it
#   conflicts:       none, onto is quiet
#   emission:        rename /proj to /work; inside that scope, unlink
#                    conf's predecessor and link conf; data needs no
#                    edit, its relativized name never changed

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: proj/
      3-100: cfg/
      5-100: data/

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: proj/
      3-100: cfg/
      5-100: data/

tree offof
  1-1: /
    10-300: work/
      11-300: conf/
      13-300: data/

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: work
      11-300: conf/
      5-100: data/
